  "step_3": "The decision reason requires (1) computing an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes, and (2) comparing lotteries based on the probability of exceeding this level. The individual does not mention or imply such a computation. Instead, their reasoning is based on risk aversion and the attractiveness of certainty, especially given the relatively small upside of Lottery B. While both the reason and the individual prefer Lottery A, the underlying justification differs: the reason relies on a specific calculation of an aspiration level and evaluates probabilities of exceeding it; the individual relies on intuitive risk-benefit trade-off and certainty. There is no evidence that the individual computed or used the mean of all outcomes as a reference point. The considerations in the report (certainty, risk of zero, marginal gain) do not align with the informational basis or rule of the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

